vscode: Replace All in Files, twice, then git diff to find what it broke
vimrc: set nohidden
open: README.md

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    PAY-732  PaymentService.sendRefund is misnamed: it sends nothing, it
             asks the gateway to issue a refund. Rename it issueRefund
             wherever code refers to it, test names included. The
             gateway endpoint it posts to is still /sendRefund, the
             payments exchange still routes on payment.sendRefund, and
             the gateway timing metric keeps its name: dashboards are
             built on it. While you are in there, the error code it
             throws becomes PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED, in the code and in
             the error table.

Two renames. The first has four hits that must survive and no pattern
that cleanly separates them from the nine that must change, so it wants
a look at the list and then a confirm. The second crosses a `.json`
file, so `**/*.ts` will not find all of it. The README documents the
endpoint and the routing key and is correct as it stands; the changelog
records the release that introduced `REFUND_FAILED` and is history.
Neither changes.

`hidden` is off, as it is in a stock vim.

@keys
| `:vimgrep` `:copen` | find, then read before you act |
| `:cdo s/old/new/gc \| update` | change each hit with a prompt |
| `:grep -r name src` | every file type under `src`, into the same list |
| `:cdo s/old/new/g \| update` | change each hit blind, when the list is clean |

@hints
`:vimgrep /sendRefund/ **/*.ts` then `:copen`. Thirteen hits. Four of
them are inside quotes: the path twice, the routing key, the metric.
--
`:cdo s/sendRefund/issueRefund/gc | update` and answer each prompt. The
prompt shows the line, so a quoted string is easy to spot. In list
order: the order test (four, all yes), the order provider (yes), the
producer (no), the payment provider (yes), the service test (yes, no,
yes), the service (yes, no, no).
--
The error code: `:grep -r REFUND_FAILED src` reaches the json as well
as the code, and nothing under `src` should keep the old name, so a
plain `:cdo s/REFUND_FAILED/PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED/g | update` is safe.
`:qa` at the end because the quickfix window is open.
--
Par assumes one confirm pass for the method and one blind pass for the
code. A narrower pattern can take some of the load: `sendRefund[^'.]`
drops the path and the routing key, but it also drops the test's
`.sendRefund.mockResolvedValue`, so the confirm is needed either way.

@solution
    :vimgrep /sendRefund/ **/*.ts<Enter>  :copen<Enter>
    :cdo s/sendRefund/issueRefund/gc | update<Enter>  y y y y y n y y n y y n n
    :grep -r REFUND_FAILED src<Enter>  :cdo s/REFUND_FAILED/PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED/g | update<Enter>
    :qa<Enter>

The whole chapter in one ticket. The first rename is the reason the
list exists: a Replace All would have renamed the gateway endpoint and
the routing key, the deploy would have gone out, and the ledger service
would have stopped receiving refund events with nothing in the logs to
say why. Reading the list before acting is the entire discipline, and
the confirm prompt is how you act on what you read.

The second rename is the reason `:grep` exists alongside `:vimgrep`:
the code and its error table are different file types, and grep does
not care. Under `src` the old code had no reason to survive, so the
blind form was correct there. Knowing when the blind form is fine is
as much a skill as knowing when it is not.

Thirteen prompts is about the limit of the confirm approach. Past
that, narrow the pattern first, as lesson 6 did, and confirm only what
the pattern cannot separate. Here the metric `'gateway.sendRefund.ms'`
and the mock `.sendRefund.mockResolvedValue` look identical to a
regex, one character either side, so some prompting was unavoidable.
Pick the pattern when the distinction is mechanical; pick the prompt
when it takes a person to see it.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.

@start docs/CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 1.4.0

- Refunds now go through the payment gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint.
  A rejected refund raises `REFUND_FAILED` with a 502.

## 1.3.2

- Fix: cancelled orders were refunded twice when the ledger retried.

